{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And there was a king in Jeshurun</b> — this alludes to the Name mentioned above, who was king over Israel when they were upright, and when all the heads of the people were gathered together, all the tribes of Israel as one. And this verse too is connected, and the meaning is that Israel would always say: the Torah that Moses commanded us shall be an inheritance for the congregation of Jacob forever — we shall not exchange it nor alter it forever. And they would say that the Name was king over Israel when our heads, our elders, our judges, and all the tribes of Israel assembled — for all of us together accepted His kingship upon ourselves from generation to generation, and we are obligated to keep His Torah and His kingship forever. And this commandment is the utterance of <b>Anokhi</b> (\"I am\"), and it is the acceptance of the yoke of the kingdom of Heaven, as I explained there (Exodus 20:2). Now He mentioned the Torah in general and the kingdom of Heaven in particular, for whoever acknowledges the kingdom of Heaven denies idolatry and acknowledges the entire Torah. And they hinted that they and their leaders accepted the kingdom of Heaven from the mouth of the Gevurah (the Almighty), and they saw that He is the King of Israel and its Redeemer, and the Torah they received from the mouth of Moses — they accepted upon themselves and upon their seed to believe in Him and to do all that He would command by order of the King, as they said (Deuteronomy 5:24), \"But you speak to us all that YHWH our Elohim will speak to you, and we will hear and do.\" And this interpretation is clear and correct in this verse, following what they said in the Gemara in tractate Rosh HaShanah (Rosh HaShanah 32b): there are three verses of kingship — \"YHWH will reign forever and ever\" (Exodus 15:18), \"YHWH his Elohim is with him, and the shout of a king is among them\" (Numbers 23:21), and \"And there was a king in Jeshurun.\" And similarly we learned in the Sifrei (Sifrei, Berakha, Shemo): \"The seat of elders is in the session below; His great Name is extolled above, as it is said, 'And there was a king in Jeshurun, when the heads of the people were assembled together, the tribes of Israel' — 'assembled' means none other than elders, as it is said (Numbers 11:16), 'Gather to Me,' etc. 'Together, the tribes of Israel' — when they are made one union and not when they are made separate unions. And so it says (Amos 9:6), 'Who builds His upper chambers in the heavens,' etc.\" And in the Midrash Rabbah (Bemidbar Rabbah 15:18) they likewise said: as it were, His throne is established above when Israel is made one union; therefore it says, \"Who builds His upper chambers in the heavens, and His aguddah on the earth He founded it,\" and likewise, \"And there was a king in Jeshurun, when the heads of the people assembled,\" etc. And this too, according to the interpretation I have given, they would expound that He was king over them because they assembled and all together accepted His kingship upon themselves, and so it shall be a heritage for the congregation of Jacob — and the King is the Holy One, blessed be He. And so we are accustomed to say in the prayer of the school of Rav. But there are some aggadot that interpret it of Moses, saying: Moses was king, as it is said, \"And there was a king in Jeshurun, when they assembled,\" etc., and if so, the verse says, \"Moses commanded us a Torah, to be a heritage for the congregation of Jacob,\" and he was king over us and over all our tribes together, and it is fitting for us to do his words and his commandments, for he was a great and wise king over us — the verse also recounts the glory of the emissary, that he was great and worthy of being believed. But the correct and true interpretation is what we have explained. Now He has mentioned that the Name dwells among Israel, and they have accepted His Torah and His kingship, all their tribes together, upon themselves and upon their seed for their generations; if so, they are worthy of blessing."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :register {:flags {0 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {0 "ע\"ז is expanded as עבודה זרה (idolatry). הקב\"ה is expanded as הקדוש ברוך הוא (the Holy One, blessed be He). The abbreviation ס\"א / וג\"כ is rendered as a citation note; it appears to indicate a variant reading or 'and if so.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:59:09.274686246Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}